iþ5›ÀÇÊƒü|r›*!ÑÙ…V`ÿ/T_b›4O{"url":"/gcsl/embedding/definition-types/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/embedding/definition-types/","content":"Definition Types and Metadata. A definition type lets a host or library describe a family of named functions. The language checks each definition against a shared signature, while the host can discover and invoke definitions by name. This is the mechanism used by integrations such as Pekit. A family of definitions # Mark a public function with #[def_type]. Its first parameter receives the definition's path as a string; its second receives the definition function. Further parameters become invocation arguments. This example wraps a pure string-producing definition: Copy#[def_type] def describe(name: String, body: fn(): String): String { \"{name}: {body()}\" } def describe dev.peios.gcsl() { \"language tools\" } result = describe dev.peios.gcsl() Declaring the definition does not execute its body. Calling its reference does. Definitions live in the definition type's namespace, separate from ordinary values of the same path. Their public paths merge through workspace directories and imports like other public declarations. Definitions made with a variant # A definition type can take a variant of an enum after the name, and the definition after that. Each definition then names a variant, and each variant has its own definitions. When the definition type is generic, the variant's payload type fills in its type parameter, so each definition's body is checked against its own variant's payload: Copyenum Tool extensible<{*: String}> enum Tool::C<{cc: String}> enum Tool::Rust<{rustc: String}> #[def_type] def Tool<T>(name: String, kind: Variant<Tool, T>, body: fn(): T): Tool { kind(body()) } def Tool::C gcc() { cc = \"gcc\" } def Tool::Rust gcc() { rustc = \"rustc\" } c = Tool::C gcc() rust = Tool::Rust gcc() Tool::C gcc and Tool::Rust gcc are separate definitions. A reference names the variant in the same way as the definition, and the definition type receives the variant as its kind argument. Hosts don't invoke these definitions by name: programs reach them through references, for example in a list of registered toolchains. Metadata # Function types can carry metadata with META:, META->, or META->>. These distinguish pure reads, impure reads, and memoized reads. A defmeta declaration supplies the metadata of a definition in its directory. Reading a field through the function reference reads that metadata according to its effect. Metadata is not a separate package-format contract. The definition type chooses its shape, and the host integration owns what those fields mean. Use the host's documentation for package or build semantics. Host invocation # A checked workspace exposes definition types and their definitions. Host APIs can invoke a selected definition with named textual arguments parsed against its declared parameter types. When a value the host gets back holds a reference, such as build = build busybox, the host can ask which definition it names and then invoke it, or read its metadata, by name. The gcsl executable itself exposes run, check, fmt, and lsp; it does not add a general command for each definition type. Keep protocol or package policy in the integration repository. Keep language syntax, type checking, and discovery semantics in GCSL. This separation lets other hosts use the same language without inheriting Pekit's packaging model.","word_count":506,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / Embedding GCSL","title":"Definition Types and Metadata"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"a-family-of-definitions","text":"A family of definitions #","location":47},{"element":"h2","id":"definitions-made-with-a-variant","text":"Definitions made with a variant #","location":149},{"element":"h2","id":"metadata","text":"Metadata #","location":311},{"element":"h2","id":"host-invocation","text":"Host invocation #","location":389}]}},"word_data":{"through":[{"position":140,"weight":